Certainly! Below is a simple example of a 4-bit adder in Verilog. This adder takes two 4-bit inputs, `a` and `b`, and produces a 4-bit sum `s` along with a carry-out bit `c_out`.

```verilog
module adder_4bit (
    input [3:0] a,  // 4-bit input
    input [3:0] b,  // 4-bit input
    output [3:0] s, // 4-bit sum
    output c_out   // carry-out
);
    // Full adder module
    module full_adder (
        input a,
        input b,
        input c_in,
        output sum,
        output c_out
    );
        assign {c_out, sum} = a + b + c_in;
    endmodule

    // 4-bit adder using full adders
    wire [3:0] c;
    full_adder fa0 (.a(a[0]), .b(b[0]), .c_in(0), .sum(s[0]), .c_out(c[0]));
    full_adder fa1 (.a(a[1]), .b(b[1]), .c_in(c[0]), .sum(s[1]), .c_out(c[1]));
    full_adder fa2 (.a(a[2]), .b(b[2]), .c_in(c[1]), .sum(s[2]), .c_out(c[2]));
    full_adder fa3 (.a(a[3]), .b(b[3]), .c_in(c[2]), .sum(s[3]), .c_out(c_out));
endmodule
```

This code defines a 4-bit adder using a full adder module. The full adder handles the addition of two bits along with a carry-in bit and produces a sum and a carry-out bit. The 4-bit adder uses four full adders connected in series to handle the 4-bit addition.
